        // To trigger the difference, the external call must fail. C's hardcoded target
        // is an EOA that normally succeeds. We must deploy code at that hardcoded
        // address to control the outcome. We use vm.etch to place our helper's bytecode
        // at the hardcoded address. This is the only way to make C's hardcoded target
        // adversarial without modifying C.
        // The ADVERSARIAL COUNTERPARTY rule requires deploying a helper and using its
        // address where C expects the counterparty. Since C hardcodes the address,
        // we use vm.etch to place the helper's code at that address.
        address hardcodedTarget = 0xC8A60C51967F4022BF9424C337e9c6F0bD220E1C;
        vm.etch(hardcodedTarget, address(callee).code);
        // Set up the caller with ETH
        address caller = makeAddr("caller");
        vm.deal(caller, amount);
        vm.prank(caller);
        // We need to capture the contract balance after the call.
        // If the external call fails and the require is present, the whole go() reverts,
        // and no ETH is sent. The contract balance remains 0.
        // If the require is absent, the call fails, but execution continues and
        // owner.transfer(address(this).balance) is called. The contract balance at that
        // point is whatever it had before go() (which is 0) plus msg.value (which was
        // sent to the target, but target reverted, so the value stays in the contract).
        // Wait: call{value: msg.value} to a reverting target: the ETH is not sent,
        // it remains in the calling contract. So after the failed call, address(this).balance
        // is msg.value. Then owner.transfer(address(this).balance) sends all of it to owner.
        // So owner receives the amount. So after go() succeeds (no revert), owner has the amount,
        // contract balance is 0.
        // If the require is present and the call fails, go() reverts, nothing changes.
        // So the difference is: with require, go() reverts on call failure, with mutant it succeeds
        // and owner gets the amount, contract balance becomes 0.
        // So the property: if go() does not revert, contract balance must be 0.
        // The mutant always succeeds, so contract balance is always 0 after go().
        // The reference reverts on call failure, so contract balance remains 0 (unchanged).
        // Hmm. The statement says "the value of state variable `go` differs: P.go() != M.go()".
        // But go() doesn't return anything. They probably mean the observable state.
        // Actually the difference described: "after a call to go(...) completes, the value of state variable `go` differs" - go is a function, not a state variable. Possibly they mean the contract balance or the owner's balance.
        // Given the mutant: remove require, so failed calls don't revert, changing the contract balance sent to owner.
        // So the difference is: in reference, if call fails, go reverts, nothing sent to owner.
        // In mutant, if call fails, go still sends contract balance to owner.
        // We can assert: if the call fails, go() must revert.
        bool ok = true;
        try c.go{value: amount}() {
            // succeeded
        } catch {
            ok = false;
        }
        // Property: if the hardcoded target reverts, go() must revert.
        // The mutant will not revert, so ok will be true even when shouldFail is true.
        // The reference will revert when shouldFail is true, making ok false.
        // We need to assert that ok == false when shouldFail is true.
        // But the fuzzer supplies shouldFail. The property must hold for ALL inputs.
        // So we assert: if Callee was set to fail, go() must revert.
        // However, we need a property that holds for ALL admissible inputs.
        // We can assert: (shouldFail == true) => (ok == false)
        // That is equivalent to: !shouldFail || !ok
        // In Solidity: assertTrue(!shouldFail || !ok);
        assertTrue(!shouldFail || !ok);
    }
}

contract Callee {
    bool shouldRevert;
    constructor(bool _shouldRevert) {
        shouldRevert = _shouldRevert;
    }
    fallback() external payable {
        if (shouldRevert) {
            revert("fail");
        }
    }
}

